## Change Requests
- [00:43.0] Remove or replace the glorified quote "The Layer" in the Intelligence Configuration section.
- [01:13.0] Vary the layout structures; avoid repeating the three-block structure in every section.
- [02:37.0] Utilize the white space better in the Adoption and Automation section.
- [02:48.0] Improve text placement in the Adoption and Automation section.
- [03:10.0] Redesign the flow diagram in Adoption and Automation; use proper diagrams instead of a "children's drawing" or "shitty mockup".
- [03:51.0] Remove rounded shapes and doodle arrows from the timeline section to align with the brand system.
- [04:39.0] Rethink the visual for explaining "The Layer" built through adoption to convey the concept clearly.
- [05:55.0] Fix the narrative transition into the "Loop decided to go AI-first" section so it connects better to previous content.
- [07:22.0] Remove the "Made in Claude" text and date; emphasize the client name instead.
- [07:41.0] Improve spacing and information architecture in the ad card to prevent it from looking crammed.
- [08:44.0] Stop breaking down sentences with arbitrarily large fonts (e.g., "0 frames", "Weeks"); follow standard typography practices.
- [09:41.0] Use actual visual cluster maps where appropriate instead of just text lists.
- [10:10.0] Add a narrative proposition to the "Plugin in your Claude Enterprise" section.

## Synthesis
Vince Buyssens is looking for a more refined, brand-aligned, and narratively cohesive design for the proposal page. He strongly dislikes elements that feel out of place or low-fidelity, explicitly rejecting doodle arrows, rounded shapes, and "children's drawing" aesthetics in the diagrams. He is critical of poor space utilization, repetitive layout structures (like relying entirely on a 3-block grid), and sloppy typography practices, particularly the AI-generated pattern of breaking sentences apart with arbitrarily oversized fonts. 

His core requirement is a complete overhaul of how the central proposition—"The Layer" and its integration into workflows via adoption and automation—is communicated visually. He wants professional, clean information architecture and proper diagrams that clearly tell the story, viewing the current mockups as inadequate. 

Furthermore, he emphasizes the importance of a smooth narrative flow between sections, noting that transitions currently feel abrupt or nonsensical. Finally, he wants generic UI patterns (like prominent "Made in Claude" stamps or irrelevant dates) removed in favor of highlighting the client and maintaining strict adherence to their own brand system. He views the first two core proposition sections as the highest priority to fix before tackling the rest of the page.

